

{"id":"3116668e-c0af-4455-aa62-70352a180e9b","object":"chat.completion","created":1786438012,"model":"deepseek-v4-pro","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"record_id\":\"CA12_CORE_012\",\"decisions\":[{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_01\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_02\",\"held\":false,\"path_id\":\"PATH_08\",\"depart_slot\":12,\"charge_blocks\":0},{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_02\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_01\",\"held\":false,\"path_id\":\"PATH_04\",\"depart_slot\":8,\"charge_blocks\":0},{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_03\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_01\",\"held\":false,\"path_id\":\"PATH_06\",\"depart_slot\":12,\"charge_blocks\":0},{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_04\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_02\",\"held\":false,\"path_id\":\"PATH_07\",\"depart_slot\":7,\"charge_blocks\":1},{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_05\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_01\",\"held\":false,\"path_id\":\"PATH_09\",\"depart_slot\":18,\"charge_blocks\":0}]}","reasoning_content":"We are given a complex UAV scheduling problem with repairs. We need to output a single JSON object with a list of decisions for all missions. The baseline decisions are provided for reference, but we need to adjust due to interventions (closures, overrides, etc.). The task is to optimize lexicographically: urgent holds, total holds, repair distance, weighted tardiness, delay plus charge wait, then energy.\n\nFirst, understand the interventions:\n- Charger capacity overrides: none.\n- Closed corridor slots: COR_04 is closed from slot 10 to 33 inclusive. Also COR_03 is closed from slot 7 to 30 inclusive. That affects missions using those corridors.\n- Corridor capacity overrides: none.\n- Initial SOC overrides: UAV_02 initial_soc_kwh is 2.722988 (instead of 3.826329 from fleet). UAV_01 initial SOC remains as in fleet: 9.639227.\n- Mission window overrides: none.\n- Pad capacity overrides: VP_04 pad capacity reduced to 1 at slot 9.\n\nWe also have default capacities: corridor capacity 2, charger capacity 2 per vertiport, pad capacity 2 per vertiport except the override. Turnaround slots: 1 at destination.\n\nNormative SOC event order: For every served mission: incoming deadhead -> service flight -> reserve check -> destination turnaround -> post-service charge.\nCharge_blocks happen at destination after turnaround. They can only be used by later missions of the same UAV.\n\nUAV assignment and mission order are FIXED. From \"assignment_order_contract\": \"FIXED_UAV_ASSIGNMENT_AND_FIXED_UAV_ORDER\". So each mission is pre-assigned to a specific UAV (uav_id) and has a sequence_index (order within that UAV). So we must follow that order. UAV_01 has missions: MISSION_02 (seq 0), MISSION_03 (seq 1), MISSION_05 (seq 2). UAV_02 has missions: MISSION_04 (seq 0), MISSION_01 (seq 1). The baseline had these assignments and that must be maintained. So we cannot reassign missions to other UAVs; we must schedule them in the given sequence order per UAV.\n\n\"Incoming deadhead\" contract: FIXED_REGISTERED_INCOMING_DEADHEAD_PATH. Each mission has expected_incoming_origin_vertiport, and we may need to account for incoming deadhead energy? But missions have incoming_deadhead=null, so no incoming deadhead energy? Actually, \"incoming_deadhead\": null for all missions in the instance. So no incoming deadhead to consider. So each mission starts with UAV at its origin vertiport with the current SOC at departure. The UAV must travel from origin to destination as service flight. Then reserve check, then turnaround, then charge.\n\nBut what about the initial position? UAVs start at VP_03 with initial SOC at available_slot (7). So for the first mission of each UAV, the UAV is at VP_03 at slot 7 (available_slot). That mission's origin must be VP_03 (for UAV_01's first mission MISSION_02, origin is VP_03, good. For UAV_02's first mission MISSION_04, origin is VP_03, good). Subsequent missions: after finishing a mission, the UAV is at the destination of that mission. Then before the next mission, there might be deadhead to reposition to the origin of the next mission? The contract says FIXED_REGISTERED_INCOMING_DEADHEAD_PATH and all missions have incoming_deadhead = null, so presumably no repositioning deadhead is allowed? Actually, in many such problems, if the UAV is not at the origin, it might need to deadhead. But the deadhead_contract says \"FIXED_REGISTERED_INCOMING_DEADHEAD_PATH\" which might mean that we are given a specific deadhead path for each mission, but here all are null, so there is no deadhead. Therefore, the UAV must already be at the origin vertiport for the mission to start; if it ends the previous mission at a different vertiport, it cannot serve the next mission because no repositioning is allowed. That seems restrictive. But looking at baseline: MISSION_02 (UAV_01) from VP_03 to VP_04, then MISSION_03 (UAV_01) from VP_04 to VP_05. After MISSION_02, UAV_01 is at VP_04, which matches origin of MISSION_03. Then MISSION_05 from VP_05 to VP_02. So chain works. For UAV_02: MISSION_04 from VP_03 to VP_04, then MISSION_01 from VP_04 to VP_01. So no deadhead needed. So it seems the sequence is already designed so that destination of previous = origin of next. So we just need to schedule those flights with timing.\n\nNow, need to ensure constraints: release slots, due slots, path options compatibility, corridor closures, capacities, energy, charging, etc.\n\nLet's list missions with details:\n\nUAV_01 missions in sequence: \n1. MISSION_02: release 7, due 30. Origin VP_03, destination VP_04. Path options: only PATH_04 (COR_02, distance 1.11 km, travel_slots 2, wind 1, energy 0.139227). Compatible layers L120_220, UAV_01 compatible with L120_220, L220_320. Max wind level 1, wind level on this path is 1, OK. UAV_01 payload_capacity 4.0 kg, payload 0.5 kg, OK. \n2. MISSION_03: release 10, due 13 (urgent). Origin VP_04, dest VP_05. Path options: PATH_01 (COR_04, 2 slots, wind 1, energy 0.130447), PATH_03 (COR_07, 4 slots, wind 0, 0.23504), PATH_06 (COR_01, 1 slot, wind 1, 0.130447). Note: COR_04 is closed from slot 10 onward. If we depart at or after release 10, COR_04 is closed, so PATH_01 cannot be used if its travel occurs in closed slots. We'll check slots. PATH_06 uses COR_01, which is open, travel_slots=1, energy same as PATH_01. PATH_03 uses COR_07, travel_slots=4, more energy. We'll prefer shorter travel to meet due date? But due slot 13 is tight. We can also hold missions if needed.\n3. MISSION_05: release 18, due 27. Origin VP_05, dest VP_02. Only PATH_09 (COR_09, 1 slot, wind 0, energy 0.24295).\n\nUAV_02 missions:\n1. MISSION_04: release 7, due 9 (urgent). Origin VP_03, dest VP_04. Path options: PATH_02 (COR_03, 2 slots, wind 1, energy 0.200697), PATH_05 (COR_05, 3 slots, wind 1, energy 0.169821), PATH_07 (COR_08, 2 slots, wind 1, energy 0.150523). Note: COR_03 is closed from slot 7 to 30. So PATH_02 can't be used if travel occurs during closed slots. Release is 7, so departure must be >=7. If we depart at 7, travel slots 2 occupy slots 7 and 8? Need to define slot occupancy: The edge_segments have duration_slots, offset_slot 0. Typically, travel occupies slots starting from depart_slot. So if depart_slot = 7 and travel_slots = 2, the UAV is in the corridor during slots 7 and 8? Or does it mean the flight takes 2 full slots, so arrival at depart_slot + travel_slots? Usually, \"travel_slots\" is the number of slots from departure to arrival. If depart_slot is the slot when the UAV leaves origin, then arrival slot = depart_slot + travel_slots? But careful: The baseline had depart_slot values: MISSION_04 depart 7, travel 2 (PATH_02), so arrival at 9? But due slot is 9, so arrival at 9 is okay. In baseline, they used PATH_02, but that's now impossible because COR_03 closed starting slot 7. So we need to use another path. PATH_05 uses COR_05, travel_slots 3, not closed. PATH_07 uses COR_08, travel_slots 2, not closed. But need to check wind level: both wind 1, UAV_02 max wind 2, okay. Energy: PATH_07 energy 0.150523, PATH_05 energy 0.169821. UAV_02 initial SOC is overridden to 2.722988. For MISSION_04, empty energy + payload energy? The energy_kwh given in path options likely includes both empty and payload? Let's check: The fleet parameters: empty_energy_kwh_per_km, payload_energy_kwh_per_km_kg. Typically, path energy_kwh is total energy for the flight, computed as (empty_energy + payload_energy * payload_kg) * distance. For UAV_02, empty_energy 0.145, payload_energy 0.012 per km per kg, payload 0.5 kg, so total per km = 0.145 + 0.012*0.5 = 0.145 + 0.006 = 0.151 per km. PATH_07 distance 0.936 km => energy = 0.151 * 0.936 = 0.141336, but given energy is 0.150523. Slight difference? Might be due to wind multiplier? Wind energy multiplier per level: UAV_02 wind_energy_multiplier_per_level 0.065. So with wind level 1, the energy is multiplied by (1 + 0.065) = 1.065. So 0.151*0.936*1.065 = 0.151*0.936=0.141336 *1.065=0.150523, matches. So we can trust given energy values. So energy consumption for each path is as given, incorporating wind.\n\nReserve check: After service flight, SOC must be >= reserve_kwh. Reserve for UAV_02: 2.5 kWh. So initial SOC 2.722988 - flight energy must be >= 2.5? That means max energy allowed is 0.222988 kWh. PATH_07 energy 0.150523, so reserve okay. PATH_05 energy 0.169821, also okay. Path_02 energy 0.200697 also okay. So energy is fine for reserve. Then after reserve, we can charge.\n\nNow timing: For MISSION_04 due slot 9, urgent. We need to depart as early as possible, but release is 7. With PATH_07 (2 slots), depart at 7, arrival slot 9? If depart_slot is 7, travel_slots=2, arrival slot = 7+2=9. That is exactly due slot 9 (assuming arrival at slot 9 is okay, due slot usually means must arrive by end of that slot?). So that works. PATH_05 (3 slots) would arrive at 10, violating due date. So we need to use PATH_07 to meet due date 9. But we must check corridor capacity for COR_08: no closures, capacity 2, likely fine. Pad capacity: origin VP_03 at slot 7: pad capacity 2, but UAV_02 also at VP_03 at slot 7? Initial both UAVs at VP_03 available slot 7. That's two UAVs, pad capacity 2, so ok if they both depart at 7? But need to check if any other usage. Also UAV_01 MISSION_02 departs at 7? Baseline had UAV_01 MISSION_02 depart at 7. That mission also from VP_03. So both UAVs would be at VP_03 at slot 7. That's 2 pads, exactly capacity. So no issue. Additionally, there is an override: pad capacity VP_04 at slot 9 is 1. But MISSION_04 arrives at VP_04 at slot 9. So we need to check VP_04 pad capacity at slot 9. MISSION_04 arrival slot 9, it will occupy a pad at VP_04 during slot 9? Typically, the UAV is at destination for turnaround and charging. So it will be on ground at VP_04 from arrival slot until departure of next mission. Pad capacity is per slot. At slot 9, only MISSION_04 arrives (UAV_02). Is UAV_01 also at VP_04 at slot 9? UAV_01 MISSION_02 departs VP_03 at 7, travel 2 slots, arrival at VP_04 at slot 9 as well (PATH_04 has travel_slots 2). So both UAV_01 and UAV_02 would arrive at VP_04 at slot 9. But pad capacity at VP_04 slot 9 is only 1 due to override. That means we cannot have both arriving at slot 9! That's a problem. So we need to adjust departure times to avoid exceeding pad capacity.\n\nIn baseline, MISSION_04 (UAV_02) used PATH_02 with depart 7, arrival 9; MISSION_02 (UAV_01) used PATH_04 depart 7, arrival 9. Both arriving at VP_04 at slot 9. But baseline had pad capacity override? The intervention includes pad_capacity_overrides with slot 9, VP_04 capacity 1. So baseline was before this override. With the override, we must adjust. So we need to stagger their arrivals, or hold one mission? But MISSION_04 is urgent due 9, cannot delay. MISSION_02 is routine due 30, can delay. But MISSION_02 release is 7. Could we delay MISSION_02's departure to make its arrival slot >9? PATH_04 travel 2 slots, so depart at 8 would arrive at 10 (if slots 8 and 9 travel? Actually, if depart_slot=8, travel 2 slots, arrival slot = 8+2 = 10). Then VP_04 pad at slot 9: only MISSION_04 would arrive at slot 9, so capacity 1 ok. But we must check corridor closure for PATH_04? PATH_04 uses COR_04? Wait, PATH_04 uses COR_02, not COR_04. So open. But need to check if MISSION_02 can depart at 8? release is 7, so yes. But also have to consider UAV_01's subsequent missions. Also check energy: MISSION_02 energy 0.139227, UAV_01 initial SOC 9.639227, reserve 1.5, so plenty enough.\n\nNow check MISSION_04 arrival: we want depart at 7, arrival 9, pad VP_04 slot 9 is one. So if only MISSION_04 uses that pad at slot 9, ok. But MISSION_02 must not be at VP_04 at slot 9. So delay MISSION_02 departure to 8, arrival 10. Then VP_04 at slot 10: capacity default 2. So after slot 9, pad capacity returns to 2. So MISSION_02 arrives at 10, MISSION_04 is still at VP_04 from slot 9 onward (it will need turnaround and charging, and then depart for next mission). For the next missions: UAV_01 after MISSION_02 needs to do MISSION_03 from VP_04 to VP_05. UAV_02 after MISSION_04 needs to do MISSION_01 from VP_04 to VP_01. So both will be at VP_04 after their respective arrivals, and they will need to depart from VP_04. So we must manage departure slots to avoid pad conflicts at VP_04.\n\nNow, MISSION_04 is urgent due 9, so arrival at 9 is fine. After MISSION_04, UAV_02 must do MISSION_01 (sequence index 1). MISSION_01 is from VP_04 to VP_01, release slot 10, due slot 25. So we need to schedule MISSION_01. But first, after MISSION_04 arrival at 9, we need turnaround (1 slot) and then optional charge. Turnaround: \"destination turnaround\" occurs after reserve check, then post-service charge. The event order: incoming deadhead (none), service flight (MISSION_04), reserve check, destination turnaround (1 slot), then charge. So after arrival, the UAV is at destination. The reserve check happens immediately after flight. Then turnaround takes 1 slot? I need to understand the timeline: The departure slot for a mission is the slot when it leaves origin. The arrival slot is depart_slot + travel_slots. At the arrival slot, the UAV is on the ground? Probably yes. Then turnaround occupies the next slot(s)? It says \"destination turnaround\" after reserve check. Typically, after arrival, there is a mandatory turnaround time before the UAV can be used for the next flight. The turnaround_slots is 1 in instance. So after arriving at slot 9, the UAV must spend one slot for turnaround, so it becomes available for departure earliest at slot 10? That matches MISSION_01 release slot 10. So if we want to depart at 10, we can. But we also might want to charge. However, MISSION_01 energy requirements? MISSION_01 path only PATH_08: distance 0.832 km, energy 0.125632. UAV_02 SOC after MISSION_04: initial 2.722988 - 0.150523 = 2.572465, reserve check: 2.572465 >= 2.5, okay. After turnaround, we can charge. To depart for MISSION_01, we need enough energy: flight energy 0.125632 + reserve 2.5 = 2.625632 needed? Or reserve check happens after service flight, so before departure for MISSION_01, we need SOC at departure >= flight energy + reserve? Actually, reserve check occurs after the service flight. So the UAV must have at departure enough energy to complete the flight and still have reserve left. So minimum departure SOC = flight_energy + reserve_kwh. For UAV_02, reserve 2.5. So minimum departure SOC for MISSION_01 = 0.125632 + 2.5 = 2.625632. Current SOC after MISSION_04 (post-flight, before charge) is 2.572465, which is less than 2.625632. So we must charge before MISSION_01 to raise SOC above threshold. We have charge_kwh_per_block 0.95 kWh per block. So we can add charge blocks after turnaround. Each block of charging likely occupies some time? The problem says \"post-service charge\" and \"charge_blocks\" attached to a mission occurs at that mission's destination after its service flight and turnaround. It does not explicitly define how many slots a charge block occupies. But from typical formulations, charging takes a certain number of slots per block? The fleet has charge_kwh_per_block but no mention of time per block. However, in the baseline, for MISSION_04, charge_blocks=0. For MISSION_01, charge_blocks=0. So maybe charging doesn't consume slots? Possibly charging is instantaneous? But that seems odd given the emphasis on charger capacity. The description: \"charge_blocks attached to a mission occurs at that mission's destination after its service flight and the stated turnaround. It cannot power that mission's incoming deadhead, service flight, or reserve check. Its energy is available only to later missions assigned to the same UAV. At the start of a later mission, all legal post-service charging completed after earlier missions is already included in the UAV's available energy. Check reserve immediately after incoming-deadhead plus service energy are deducted. Check the battery upper bound immediately after post-service charging. Charger capacity applies to every occupied post-service charging slot at the destination.\" So charging does take slots, because charger capacity applies to every occupied post-service charging slot. So each charge block takes some number of slots? It doesn't explicitly say. But likely, a charge block takes 1 slot? Or maybe the number of slots is equal to the number of blocks? We need to infer from the instance parameters. There is \"max_charge_blocks\": 3, but no \"slots_per_charge_block\". In many such problems, a charge block corresponds to one slot of charging at the charger. But the energy added per block is 0.95 for UAV_02, so if you charge for 1 slot you get 0.95 kWh. So we need to allocate charge blocks as integer, and each block occupies 1 charger slot. So the UAV will be at the charger for the number of slots equal to charge_blocks. During those slots, it is occupying a charger at the vertiport, and charger capacity is per slot. Also, the UAV cannot depart for the next mission until after charging and turnaround. So the departure slot for the next mission must be >= arrival + turnaround + charge_blocks (if charging is after turnaround, the UAV must finish charging before departure).\n\nNow, we need to determine the timeline precisely: For a mission with depart_slot D, travel_slots T, arrival slot is D + T. Then turnaround: it takes the next S_slots where S = turnaround_slots = 1. So the UAV is in turnaround during slot D+T+1? Or is it that turnaround is considered part of the ground time and the UAV becomes available at D+T+S? Typically, if turnaround is 1 slot, then after arrival at slot A, the turnaround is the next slot, so available at A+1. But we also have charge blocks. If we charge, we may charge after turnaround, which would take additional slots. So the UAV would be at the charger during slots A+S+1 to A+S+charge_blocks (assuming 1 block per slot). Then the next mission can depart at slot D_next >= A+S+charge_blocks? Or maybe they can depart immediately after the last charge slot? So D_next >= A + S + charge_blocks. So the number of slots between departure of consecutive missions (same UAV) = travel_slots of first mission + S + charge_blocks + (maybe some idle time). Also, note that the next mission's release slot may be higher, so we can insert idle.\n\nNow, for UAV_02 after MISSION_04: arrival at 9, S=1, so turnaround done by end of slot 10? Actually, if turnaround occupies slot 10, then available at start of slot 11? But let's think in terms of slot numbers: A=9, if turnaround takes 1 slot, then during slot 10, the UAV is in turnaround. So it cannot depart until slot 11. However, release for MISSION_01 is 10. So earliest possible departure is 11 if no charging. But we need charging anyway. So let's compute minimum departure slot for MISSION_01. We need enough SOC. SOC after MISSION_04 flight: 2.572465. Required for MISSION_01: 2.625632. So need at least ceil((2.625632 - 2.572465)/0.95) blocks? Difference = 0.053167, so 1 block gives 0.95, more than enough. So 1 charge block adds 0.95 kWh, new SOC = 3.522465 (subject to battery capacity 22 kWh, well under). So we need 1 charge block. That takes one slot. So UAV_02: arrival 9, turnaround slot 10, charge slot 11? Actually, order: service flight -> reserve check (implicit) -> destination turnaround -> post-service charge. So after landing at slot 9, we do reserve check, then turnaround begins. Turnaround duration 1 slot, so turnaround occupies slot 10. Then charge occurs after turnaround, so charging would start at slot 11, and if 1 block, it would be in slot 11. Then UAV available at slot 12. So earliest departure for MISSION_01 is slot 12. But release is 10, so that's okay. However, we could also hold on ground without charging? But we need charge, so have to charge. So MISSION_01 depart_slot >= 12. Due slot 25, so plenty of time.\n\nNow, concern: pad capacity at VP_04 during these slots. UAV_02 will be on pad at VP_04 from arrival slot 9 until departure of MISSION_01 (or until after charging?). The UAV occupies a pad during ground time (not while in flight). So when it is in turnaround and charging, it's on a pad/charger. The problem states: pad_capacity_by_slot for vertiport, which includes both pads and chargers? Probably pad capacity is for parking positions, and chargers are a subset of pads? But there are separate charger capacities. Typically, each charger is a resource, and a UAV during charging uses a charger and also a pad. But \"pad_capacity_by_slot\" is defined, and \"charger_capacity_by_slot\" separate. So likely a vertiport has pad slots (parking spaces) and charger slots (charging stations). A UAV that is on the ground but not charging occupies only a pad. While charging, it occupies both a pad and a charger. So we need to respect both capacities.\n\nThus, UAV_02 at VP_04 from slot 9 to slot 11 (arrival 9, turnaround 10, charge 11) will occupy a pad during those slots. And during slot 11, it also occupies a charger. Then at slot 12 it departs (if depart 12), so it's gone after departure. So pad occupancy: slots 9,10,11. UAV_01 will have MISSION_02 arriving at VP_04 at slot 10 (if we depart at 8, travel 2, arrival 10). So UAV_01 arrives at 10, occupies pad from slot 10 onwards (turnaround, charge if any, then next departure). At slot 10, UAV_02 already occupies a pad since slot 9. So total pads used at VP_04 slot 10: UAV_02 (arrived 9) + UAV_01 (arrived 10) = 2. Default pad capacity is 2, and override only restricted slot 9 to 1. So at slot 10, capacity is 2 again. So okay. At slot 9, only UAV_02 occupies pad, capacity 1, okay. So staggering arrivals works. However, we also need to consider if UAV_01's arrival at 10 might conflict with UAV_02's ground operations? The pad capacity is per slot, so as long as total ground UAVs <= capacity, fine.\n\nNow UAV_01 MISSION_02: depart slot 8, travel 2 slots (PATH_04), arrival slot 10. Turnaround 1 slot -> slot 11. Then MISSION_03 of UAV_01: sequence next. MISSION_03 from VP_04 to VP_05, release 10, due 13, urgent. We need to schedule it. UAV_01 will be at VP_04 after arrival 10. Turnaround takes slot 11, so available earliest dep slot 12. MISSION_03 has due 13, so depart 12, travel to VP_05 must arrive by 13. Paths: PATH_06 uses COR_01, travel 1 slot; if depart 12, arrival 13, exactly due. PATH_01 uses COR_04 closed, so no. PATH_03 uses COR_07, travel 4 slots, arrival 16, too late. So only PATH_06 works for due date. But we need to check corridor capacities and other constraints. PATH_06: distance 1.04 km, energy 0.130447, wind 1 (UAV_01 max wind 1, ok). Also check if COR_01 is open; no closures for COR_01. So feasible.\n\nBut we also need to check energy: UAV_01 initial SOC 9.639227, minus MISSION_02 energy 0.139227 = 9.5 remains after MISSION_02 (before reserve check). Reserve for UAV_01 is 1.5 kWh, so SOC after flight is 9.5, well above reserve. Then after turnaround, we might charge before MISSION_03? Let's check if needed: MISSION_03 flight energy 0.130447, so departure SOC needed >= 0.130447 + 1.5 = 1.630447. We have 9.5, far a